.TH SWEEP 1
.SH NAME
sweep \- a quiet disk cleaner for macOS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sweep
.br
.B sweep scan
.br
.B sweep items
.I group
.br
.B sweep clean
.RI [ group ]
.B --yes
.br
.B sweep clean homebrew --empty --yes
.br
.B sweep uninstall
.I Name
.RB [ --yes ]
.RB [ --leftovers ]
.br
.B sweep uninstall restore
.I Name
.br
.B "sweep walk stays"
.br
.B sweep orphans
.RB [ "remove ID --yes" ]
.br
.B sweep login
.RB [ "remove FILE --yes" ]
.br
.B sweep last
.RB [ all ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
sweep lists the largest folders it is willing to touch and waits.
Nothing is deleted until you type
.B yes
or pass
.BR --yes .
.PP
.B rebuilds
means the app or tool creates that folder again.
.B stays
means the space stays gone.
.B go
removes the safe folders on the list and skips a folder whose app is still open.
A cache whose app is gone is marked
.B stays
and is left out of
.BR go .
A leftover unused for 30 days is marked
.B stays
and is not ticked.
The first list also shows installed apps of about 100 MB or more.
Ticking one moves that app to the Trash.
.B "sweep uninstall"
lists every app, including the smaller ones.
.B "sweep uninstall restore Name"
puts the app back in the folder it came from.
Emptying the Trash from
.B go
or
.B clean
leaves an app that is sitting in the Trash.
Tick that app if you want it deleted for good.
.PP
.B go
and
.B clean
delete caches and other safe folders for good.
They do not move those folders to the Trash.
An app that is still open, or a second copy with the same bundle id, stays where it is and the menu stays open.
.B walk
prints the path under the name.
.B yes
moves an app to the Trash and deletes a backup for good.
The only backup of a device, and the only support folder for a system version, ask you to type that name before they are deleted.
A device-support folder does not boot the Mac.
.B info
on an app shows the bundle id, the team id, and the folder a restore would use.
A simulator line says the size is unknown.
.PP
.B uninstall
moves one app to the Trash.
Leftovers stay unticked unless you pass
.BR --leftovers .
A preference file is shown and is not deleted.
A shared folder, including a vendor folder such as Adobe, is shown and is never a delete list.
.PP
In the menu, type
.B uninstall
or
.B login
for a numbered list.
Nothing on those lists is selected until you type a number and then
.BR yes .
.SH FOLDERS NEVER ENTERED
Documents, Desktop, Pictures, Movies, Music, Mail, Messages, Preferences, and
.IR ~/.ssh .
CloudKit, HomeKit, and Family Sharing caches are left alone.
Crash reports in DiagnosticReports are kept.
Cookies are not wiped.
.PP
Homebrew downloads older than 120 days are the normal Homebrew clean.
.B "sweep clean homebrew --empty --yes"
empties the rest of that cache.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sweep/removed.txt
Removals that are not caches.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sweep/removed-caches.txt
Cache removals.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sweep/removed-homebrew.txt
Homebrew removals. A long cache wipe does not push this list out.
.PP
.B sweep last
prints the latest lines of each file that exists.
.B sweep last all
prints the whole of each.
Each line has the date, the size, the name, and the path.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, sweep prints plain text.
.TP
.B SWEEP_LOG_DAYS
Log files older than this many days are eligible. The default is 7.
.SH SEE ALSO
The menu in the README, and
.IR RELEASE.md .
